<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Suspected chronic rhinosinusitis in a 25-year-old private pilot with five months of nasal blockage, discolored drainage, left cheek and forehead pressure, reduced smell, and pressure pain on descent when flying. Anterior rhinoscopy showed swollen turbinates without visible polyps or pus. She started saline irrigation and fluticasone today. Please confirm the diagnosis by nasal endoscopy, say whether polyps are present, and advise on further treatment, imaging, surgery, and flying. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within two weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Does she have chronic rhinosinusitis, and are nasal polyps present?</item><item>Is saline irrigation with an intranasal steroid the right first treatment, and is there a place for an antibiotic or an oral steroid?</item><item>When should a sinus CT be done, and when would endoscopic sinus surgery help her?</item><item>Is there anything that changes management, such as allergy, immune deficiency, a dental source, or her past sinus operations?</item><item>When can she fly, which medicines can she use as a pilot, and how can she avoid sinus and ear barotrauma?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Five months of nasal blockage, thick discolored drainage, pressure over the left cheek and forehead, and a reduced sense of smell, with sleep broken two or three nights a week; pressure pain in the left cheek on descent has made her cancel four weekend flights in two months. Years ago she had a severe sinus infection during an influenza illness, treated in hospital with operations that opened her sinuses for drainage. No asthma, aspirin sensitivity, or frequent infections elsewhere; never smoked. She used oxymetazoline spray for two weeks last winter with rebound blockage, and a nighttime cold tablet containing diphenhydramine; both stopped today. Examination: swollen, red inferior turbinates; no polyps or pus seen on anterior rhinoscopy; no eye signs. Facial pain 6 of 10; SNOT-22 52. Eosinophils 0.21 x10^3/uL and total IgE 42 IU/mL, both normal. Started high-volume saline irrigation twice daily and fluticasone two sprays in each nostril daily. She holds a third-class FAA medical certificate and flies a single-engine light aircraft; we agreed she will not fly while blocked or in pain. She decides who is told about her health [S]</paragraph><list><item>Her nose is always blocked, her face aches over the left cheek and forehead, and she has lost much of her sense of smell. [S]</item><item>Pressure pain on descent has made her cancel flights, and she wants to know when it is safe to fly and which medicines she may take as a pilot. [S]</item><item>She had sinus operations after a bad infection years ago and wants to avoid another operation unless it will really help. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Amelia Mary Earhart was born on July 24, 1897, in Atchison, Kansas, to Amy Otis and Edwin Stanton Earhart. [D]</item><item>After visiting her sister in Toronto at Christmas 1917, she moved there in February 1918 and worked in the Voluntary Aid Detachment at the Spadina Military Hospital, often on twelve-hour shifts, going wherever an extra pair of hands was needed. [D]</item><item>During the 1918 influenza epidemic she worked nights on the ward that held the influenza patients; within weeks she fell ill herself, with severe sinus pain and terrible headaches. Her case was serious enough that doctors operated, opening into her sinuses so that they could be drained regularly. It was a month before she was well enough to leave hospital, and she went to recover at her sister&apos;s home in Massachusetts. [D]</item><item>She suffered from chronic sinus problems for the rest of her life. [D]</item><item>She entered Columbia University as a pre-medical student in the fall of 1919 and left in 1920. She took her first airplane ride in December 1920, with the pilot Frank Hawks, and became a licensed pilot in the early 1920s: Purdue University dates her pilot&apos;s license to May 1923, and the Encyclopedia of the Great Plains to 1921. [D]</item><item>In modern terms, sinus disease that began with a severe sinus infection during influenza, needed drainage operations, and caused lasting headaches and sinus trouble fits chronic rhinosinusitis. The fetched sources give no diagnosis in those terms, and nothing is known of her sinus anatomy or of nasal polyps. [R]</item></list><paragraph>4 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

